Nothing in Bosnian law forbids hitchhiking (''autostop''), and on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.parlament.ba/law/DownloadDocument?lawDocumentId=6dffad28-c0f8-42c5-bdfa-c55567c67370&langTag=bs Article 116(1) of the ''Zakon o osnovama sigurnosti saobraćaja na putevima u BiH''] keeps pedestrians, livestock and anything not counted as a motor vehicle off the ''autoput'', and 116(2) adds motor vehicles that cannot exceed 40 km/h. Article 117(1) lets a driver stop on a motorway only on the areas off the carriageway that are specially built and marked for it, and 117(3) forbids U-turns and reversing, so a driver who passes you cannot come back. That leaves the marked rest areas and their petrol stations as the one place on the motorway where a lift can lawfully begin. Off the motorway, article 107(1) puts a pedestrian outside a settlement on the ''left'' edge of the carriageway facing the traffic, lit or in a reflective vest after dark — which is where you stand and thumb.
